GIC-400 per-CPU CPU-interface frames (0x2000 stride) for the SGI handshake.

0152's per-PE GIC enable still left `sig=0`: CPU1 runs
sweet_secondary_entry -> initCPU (mark=0x7 init=1) but its self-IPI never
arrives.  On a GIC-400 the CPU-interface frames are 0x2000 apart in the SoC
address map; the DTB only describes CPU0's frame (0x0b002000), so our single
gicc_base meant every secondary programmed CPU0's interface.  CPU1's own
interface stayed disabled, the distributor never forwarded its self-SGI and
cpu_signal_handler() never cleared SIGPdisabled.

  pexpert/pexpert/arm64/SWEET.h
    - GICD_ISPENDR0 (0x200) for the pending diagnostic.
  pexpert/arm/pe_fiq.c
    - gicc_this (this PE's frame) + gicc_stride (default 0x2000; boot arg
      `sweetgiccoff=<n>`, 0x1000-aligned) and `sweetgiccbase=<hex>` to
      override the CPU0 frame PA;
    - pe_gic_setup_this_cpu() = gicc_base + stride*cpu
      (cpu = ml_get_cpu_number_local());
    - gicc_read32/gicc_write32 use gicc_this: pe_gic_ack(), pe_gic_eoi(), the
      per-CPU enable and the dump now hit the current PE's frame;
    - GICC window mapped 0x8000 bytes (4 frames), boot CPU's frame logged;
    - pe_gic_cpu_enable() logs `GICC=0x.. ctlr/.. pmr/.. isen/..` per CPU;
    - pe_gic_sgi_debug() logs GICC_HPPIR + GICD_ISPENDR0 for the SGI bit.
  iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetCPU.cpp
    - initCPU(): print GICC frame + readbacks before/after the enable;
    - `sweetsgidbg=1`: after the IPI is queued, one-shot
      pe_gic_sgi_debug(SGI1) (pending set but not forwarded vs forwarded and
      taken); `sweetsgidbg=2` also clears SIGPdisabled directly (old fallback);
    - timeout output: remove the bogus `cdp=` field, keep `sig_raw=0x%08x`.

SGIR targeting: with the 0x2000 stride the CPU-interface number equals the
logical CPU index, so GICD_SGIR_TARGET_LIST(1U << cpu) remains correct.

Expected: per-CPU `GICC=0x.. ctlr=0x00000003 pmr=0xff isen=0x00140002`, then
`came alive; SGI1 OK` and `SWEET CPU: 4/4 CPUs initialized`.
